{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And I appeared to Abraham, etc.</b> The reason He mentioned them in detail is that each one possesses an exalted aspect. <b>To Abraham</b> — that he was elevated in that he was the one who recognized his Creator first, without anything preceding him from on high, blessed be He. For you will find that everyone who was preceded by knowledge of the Creator from His deeds and His conduct with His creations is not to be praised when he walks in the way that Yah chose for him, for every discerning person will choose the good — and especially the perfect, pleasant, and wondrous good, which is the way of HaShem. Not so Abraham, for he cleaved to HaShem before His attributes, blessed be He, and His conduct were recognized by him. And I have already written that Abraham merited being called (Isaiah 41:8) the lover of the Place — meaning, love of truth, love of the good. For even though he had not yet recognized from Him anything but the great trials, nevertheless he cleaved to HaShem and loved Him, as we have written in its place." "<b>To Isaac.</b> — who had within him another aspect: that he stretched out his neck upon the altar." "<b>And to Jacob.</b> — who was complete, in that no despised drop departed from him, as with Ishmael and Esau." "And the intention in this is that to all three gradations of the righteous, the chosen of the world, I did not reveal myself to them except by El Shaddai, which is a revelation of a degree lower than the degree of revelation that was revealed to Moses, which is by the name YHWH, as He said, ״אֲנִי ה׳״ — and the intention in this is to inform him that he is ungrateful for the goodness of the Place, that He accounted all these so highly from among the Patriarchs, while he [Moses] speaks before Him with a lack of propriety." "And although we find that He said in the prophecy of the Patriarchs as well (Genesis 18:1) ״וַיֵּרָא אֵלָיו ה׳״, from the statement of HaShem here you learn that the verse there tells that HaShem revealed Himself to them, but they did not grasp and know this wondrous aspect — and this is His saying <b>but by My name YHWH I did not make Myself known to them</b> — meaning, this supernal aspect was not made known to them." "<b>Further</b>, it intends by His saying <b>but as for My name YHWH I did not make Myself known to them</b>, etc. — meaning: the unification of two names together, which are My name YHWH, which I made known to you, as it says ״וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים אֲנִי ה׳״, and this is the sod of what is said (I Kings 18:39) ״ה׳ הוּא הָאֱלֹהִים״, and this is the sod of what is said (Zechariah 14:9) ״ה׳ אֶחָד וּשְׁמוֹ אֶחָד״. ״I did not make Myself known to them״ — to the patriarchs — meaning: the sod of two names together, My name YHWH, which are Elohim and הויה, such that din is transformed into rachamim and rachamim into mishpat — ״I did not make Myself known״, and understand. And He said ״I did not make Myself known [לֹא נוֹדַעְתִּי]״ because YHWH blessed be He — He is His name and His name is Him, and He is not like the rest of created beings, who are separate from their names and their names are separate from them; therefore it would not be proper to say ״I did not make known [לֹא הוֹדַעְתִּי]״ because the Name itself is the One speaking, and if so one must say <b>לֹא נוֹדַעְתִּי.</b>" "<b>Further</b>, His saying <b>as El Shaddai, but My name</b> etc. may be explained along the lines of what they explained in the holy Zohar (Zohar vol. II 23a) on the verse ״וַאֲמַרְתֶּם כֹּה לֶחָי״: for the attribute of ״כֹּה״ is the aspect of the heavenly hall which is His Shekhinah, blessed be He, and its unification is חי העולמים, and this is the aspect of minor unification. Afterward He returned to unify a supernal unification and said ״וְאַתָּה שָׁלוֹם״, alluding to the exalted place which is תפארת ישראל alluded to in the letter vav. And perhaps these are the very things said here in His saying <b>as El Shaddai</b> — for this is the aspect of חי — but <b>but My name YHWH</b>, which is the unification of the Supernal, alluded to in the word ״וְאַתָּה״, which He revealed to Moses in His saying <b>וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים</b> etc. <b>אֲנִי ה׳, I did not make Myself known to them</b> etc., and understand:" "<b>Further</b>, it intends that the Master blessed be He came to answer Moses regarding His having sent him before the appointed time, and said that although He set an end to the darkness for the children of Israel to go forth from the land of Egypt, and the end has not yet been completed, you must know that the end which I set for the patriarchs is the aspect of El Shaddai, and in it I set a boundary for the exile and said to it ״enough [דַּי]״ — but another aspect ״I did not make Myself known [לֹא נוֹדַעְתִּי]״, meaning: I did not tell them the end that would come through the medium of rachamim if they would seek and arouse rachamim, as Israel did — they sought from me rachamim and knocked on the doors of rachamim before the appointed time — ״I did not make it known to them״. And therefore even though the days of affliction and distress were not completed, nevertheless I had compassion upon them through the attribute of הויה, and this is His saying at the outset <b>אֲנִי ה׳</b>, and understand." "<b>Further</b>, it intends along this line: <b>and I appeared to Abraham</b> — meaning: how could I conceal rachamim from this people, who are my children, given that I revealed myself to their fathers — and not to one alone but to all three patriarchs, each one in his time. Therefore He separated between them — ״to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob״ — and did not say ״to Abraham and Isaac and Jacob״, and this declares the praise of each one individually through El Shaddai, which is the preciousness of the revelation of the Shekhinah. And His saying <b>but as for My name YHWH I did not make Myself known to them</b> — meaning: I had not yet made known to them the measure of My rachamim, for all the promises that I promised them I had not yet brought upon them, and I desire to make known to them the measure of My rachamim, for it befits them. And HaShem intended by this to inform the greatness of their preciousness in His eyes."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:25:23.280371832Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}